WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
File names and paths are input, even when they look like ordinary strings.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:15.540
If a user, configuration file, environment variable, archive, or network message can influence a path, the program must decide what locations are allowed before opening or writing anything.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:29.120
Path traversal occurs when input can move access outside the intended directory, often through dot-dot segments, absolute paths, drive-specific syntax, encoded separators, or platform-specific path behavior.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:42.140
A path that looks harmless before normalization may resolve somewhere else after the operating system interprets it. A strong path handling design starts with an allowlisted base directory and a clear rule for relative input.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:49.480
Canonicalization can help compare the resolved path to the allowed root, but it must be used carefully and close to the operation.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:58.340
Symbolic links and time-of-check to time-of-use races can matter when attackers can modify the filesystem. Avoid treating a string check as the whole security decision.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:10.640
If the program needs to create files, consider safe creation flags, restrictive permissions, and behavior when the file already exists. Extensions are useful for user experience, but they are weak security evidence.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:20.940
An attacker can rename a file. A malformed file can carry a normal extension. When content matters, validate the actual content, expected header, or parser result.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:32.680
Also enforce file size limits before reading an entire file into memory, and handle errors without leaking sensitive filesystem details. Path input should lead to a narrow, predictable set of filesystem operations.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.260
When the program cannot prove a path is within policy, the secure answer is to reject it.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:49.520
The example is specifically for POSIX systems: it opens a trusted absolute directory with no-follow behavior, resolves one allowlisted component with openat, and verifies the opened descriptor with fstat.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:55.100
The trusted parent directories must not be attacker-controlled, and Windows requires different APIs.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:04.280
Because another writer can change a regular file after fstat, callers must either rely on protective permissions or enforce the byte limit while reading from the returned stream.
